<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Recovery custody ~ what the field has MEASURED

A tasked spirit went out over `identity-classes#the-two-forks` — Fork A ''reconstruct-then-sign'' against Fork B ''threshold-attest'' — briefed on both key scales and given no recommendation to confirm. ''It came back with a finding that cuts against the direction most of its own evidence pointed'', and with a third axis that dominates the fork on severity.

⚠ ''Held VERBATIM.'' The register is testimony, not doctrine: what a reader collides against, never what they inherit. Where the spirit corrected itself, both readings stand.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-decisive-finding>>

!! ★ The decisive comparative finding ★

> ''Every m-of-n scheme in the entire public-CA and root-zone world is SECRET SHARING — Shamir-style reconstruction — not threshold signing.''

* ''WebTrust'' defines m-of-n in terms of //"key shareholders"// and //"key shares"// — reconstruction. §4.4.1 requires //"multi-party control (i.e., m of n) with a minimum value of m (e.g., m greater than 2 for Root CAs)."//
* ''Entrust nShield'', the dominant CA HSM: recovery keys are //"split among the cards in the ACS using the same K/N mechanism as for an OCS"//, and with K cards a module can //"**re-create all the keys** stored on the device."//
* ''IANA'': 7 shares of one Storage Master Key, reassembled inside an HSM.

⚠ ''The nShield warning that reads straight onto a 2-of-3:'' //"We do not recommend creating card sets in which **K is equal to N** because an error on one card would render the whole card set unusable."// A hard cliff below K.

!!! The inversion — and it is the whole ruling

> In every one of these systems the key is ''materialized in one place at activation time'', and the quorum is ''a lock on the box, not a distributed signing protocol''. ICANN's April 2020 workaround — ''four TCRs couriering their credentials to staff surrogates'' — is only possible //because// the shares are transferable bearer secrets. ''Under threshold signing it would be meaningless.''
>
> ''Secret sharing degrades gracefully under a pandemic precisely because it can be subverted by courier. Threshold signing cannot be couriered, and therefore cannot bend.''

''Fork A's assembled secret is simultaneously its worst security property and its best availability property. They are the same property.'' Fork B's guarantee that no secret ever reconstructs is equally a guarantee that ''no guardian can ever delegate, courier, or improvise their way through a crisis'' — the guardian signs personally or the rotation does not happen.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-counterweight>>

!! ⚠ And the counterweight — Fork B carries a risk the in-house framing also missed

The courier finding cuts toward A. This cuts back:

> Independent-signature quorums are a ''DESTRUCTION'' quorum, not merely an impersonation quorum — closable, but ''only by making the rotation body canonically derived''.

Read plainly: where a recovery authority ''reconstructs'', a rogue sub-quorum can impersonate. Where it ''attests independently'', a rogue sub-quorum can also ''emit a rotation nobody intended and leave the identifier somewhere its holder never chose'' — and unless the rotation body is derived canonically rather than proposed, there is nothing for the honest guardians' signatures to disagree WITH.

''So neither branch is the safe one.'' A prices a transient assembly; B prices a rogue rotation. The mitigation named — canonical derivation of the body — is small, specific, and belongs in whichever branch is taken.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-third-axis>>

!! ⚠ The axis that dominates the one we asked about

> Under KERI's own rules, ''a non-delegated rotation cannot supersede another rotation'' — so whichever fork you take, ''a compromise of the recovery authority is terminal unless the identifier is DELEGATED''. That is a third axis, and on severity it dominates the one you asked about.

This changes the SHAPE of the decision rather than answering it: the fork prices the recovery authority's transient exposure, and delegation decides whether that exposure is ''recoverable at all''.

⚠ ''The house answered this axis without taking delegation, and #/our-own-ground carries the answer.'' The quote above states the spirit's reading; the tree settled it another way — `identity-classes:520-529` REJECTS delegation for the persona plane and the contest window (`provisional` / `vetoOfCid` / `mintVeto` / `foldPersonaContests`) stands BUILT, so a captured recovery set mints kapae-reversible authority the standing holder's own veto out-competes. Read the quote as testimony, and #/our-own-ground as the measurement.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-actually-fails>>

!! What actually fails — nineteen measured cases, and none of them is the fork

> Across five research streams, ''not one documented failure in the entire public record turned on Shamir-versus-multisig.''

They turned on, every time:

* ''quorum counts that were not counts of distinct signers'' — four sightings, one a recurrence in the same library six years later;
* ''custody correlated in ways nobody re-checked'' — Ronin's 5-of-9 that was 1-of-1, Loopring's guardians-of-guardians, FNMT's access codes stored beside the HSM;
* ''ceremonies whose script and reality diverged undetected'' for six months to six years;
* ''guardians who forgot, went dark, or folded to a warm phone call'';
* ''vendors who left''.

!!! The dominant public-CA failure mode

''Not lost quorum — the script and the executed reality diverging, undetected, for months.''

|!CA |!what went wrong |
|''IdenTrust'' 2024 |generated a ''self-signed ROOT'' meaning an intermediate — //"a PKI operator entered the wrong command parameter"// and //"the ceremony witness missed noticing the invocation of the wrong command."// ''Fix: split the tool into two binaries so the wrong object type is not selectable'' |
|''SecureTrust'' 2020 |the ceremony ''video recorder failed''; the recording was unrecoverable. ''All 7 intermediates revoked.'' The ceremony was fine; the PROOF was not |
|''SwissSign'' 2018 |the field //"had been set correctly in the key ceremony protocol"// and was mistyped in the GUI, then copy-pasted across five more CAs. ''~17 months undetected'' |
|''iTrusChina'' |root ceremony held 2018 with ''no Key Generation Ceremony audit report at all'' — //"due to our inexperience."// ''Six years undetected'', found by a root program rather than an auditor |
|''Let's Encrypt'' 2026 |''three non-compliant cross-certificates'' from a root ceremony, reported by a third party ~8 months later. //"compliance review fatigue"//. ''The CA with the best ceremony tooling in the industry still emitted three defective CA certs'' |
|''DigiCert'' 2020 |a banned SHA-1 intermediate created in ceremony — linters //"evaluate the individual certificate being signed"//, ''never the signer/signee relationship'' |

''And the offline root whose failure mode was the keyboard'' (single-source, unverified): a root-CA laptop //"sitting in a safe for five years undisturbed had its 1 key on the keyboard go bad"// — and both split-knowledge password halves contained a "1".

!!! Golden principles, each earned by an incident

* ''Make the wrong operation UNSELECTABLE, not merely discouraged.''
* ''Lint the test artifacts generated with the identical production settings, INSIDE the ceremony.''
* ''Fold compliance sign-off into the ceremony script itself.''
* ''Losing the EVIDENCE is as fatal as losing the key'' — seven intermediates revoked over a dead camcorder.
* ''Publish rehearsal artifacts in advance'' — `letsencrypt/ceremony-demos` predates the ceremony it rehearses.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-archives>>

!! The distro archives ~ nobody does threshold signing, and one project lost its key

> ''Nobody does threshold SIGNING.'' Every archive is signed by one online key. Where m-of-n appears it is in one of two adjacent places — ''RECOVERING'' a lost secret (Debian 3-of-5), or ''ADMITTING'' a key to the trust set (Debian 2 DDs, kernel.org 2 account holders, Arch 3 master keys).

* ''Debian'' — single online key on a YubiKey, ''PIN in a file beside it''. Recovery is Shamir 3-of-5; ''revocation is 1-of-5''. ⚠ ''The asymmetry is worth stealing: destroying trust is cheap and restoring it is expensive.'' Any single designated revoker can kill the archive key; it takes three to bring one back.
* ''kernel.org'' — ''no archive key at all''. The web of trust IS the recovery mechanism: ≥2 existing-member signatures re-admit a re-keyed developer. Its governing principle: //"trust must always be placed with developers and never with the code hosting infrastructure."// The 2011 recovery required ''in-person'' signatures.
* ''Kali'' — ''they lost it''. Key created 2012, never rotated, access lost 2025; ''every installation on earth fails `apt update`'' until the user manually fetches the new keyring. The whole cryptographic bridge was developers' personal keys signing the new key on a public keyserver. Precedent at the same project: they let the key ''expire by accident'' in 2018.

!!! Fedora ⊥ Red Hat, August 2008 — one intrusion, opposite calls

''Fedora'' was saved by ICANN-style split custody — //"the passphrase was not used during the time of the intrusion and is not stored on any of the Fedora servers"// — ''and rotated anyway'', because packages travel through third-party mirrors. Nine steps, ~5 weeks, ~3.5 of them with no updates at all, every package re-signed. ''Red Hat'', facing attackers who had signed real packages with a real key, ''did not rotate'' — it shipped a blacklist.

⚠ ''The bootstrap trick:'' Fedora's transitional RPMs were signed by the ''OLD'' key. The compromised-adjacent key was used one last time to establish trust in its successor. Both promised post-mortems were never published; kernel.org's promise was quietly deleted from the site.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-negative-results>>

!! The negative results ~ load-bearing

* ''No public-CA incident of lost activation shares, a lost HSM quorum card set, or an unreachable keyholder causing an inability to sign.'' Searched by phrase across the whole CA program. ⚠ ''Best read: this class happens and is only REPORTABLE if it causes a missed CRL or a misissuance'', so it is systematically absent from the record. Kali and the keyboard are the same failure surfacing where no root program compels disclosure.
* ''The only real quorum-unavailability event in public PKI is ICANN Ceremony 41''; the only physical lockout is Ceremony 40 — the safe was ''drilled'', it took ~20 hours, and //"drilling is, in fact, the manufacturer-indicated method."// ''Three key-holders sat on site for days'', because the ceremony could not proceed without them.
* ''No incident of a CA ACCIDENTALLY destroying or bricking a root key'' — only deliberate, audited destructions.
* ''The 5-of-7 IANA shares went ~14 years untested.'' The unreachable-holder scenario IS written down (KSK Key Management Procedure §6.8, ending //"the recovery key shares should be considered lost"//) — ''contemplated, never walked''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/our-own-ground>>

!! ★ Traced against OUR OWN keel — three measurements, not readings ★

The sweep names three things unmeasured at home. All three are measurable, and they change the shape of the decision.

!!! ① The system ALREADY RUNS BOTH FORKS — one per scale

* ''CIVIC scale'' — ⚠ ''corrected on a second read''. The charter ROTATION does not reconstruct anything: `sealRotate` seats the key-set that STANDS and verifies its digest against the head's `nextKeyCommit`, fail-closed. That is ''pre-rotation with a key-set reveal'' (TUF≈KERI), neither fork. ''Fork A's Shamir sits one layer beneath'' — `splitReserveSeed` 2-of-3 custodies the RESERVE SEED, the material `deriveReserveKeySet` turns into the successor key-set. So the 2-of-3 recovers ''the pre-committed future'', never the rotation authority.
* ''HUMAN scale'' (persona-KEL) runs ''Fork B'': `attestAndRotate` gathers ≥k independent guardian signatures and its own comment states the property — //"each signs with their OWN recovery key and returns a signature, and NOTHING assembles (no seed, no share)."//

So a move //off// the wired mechanism is not a move toward an unbuilt thing. ''Each scale currently holds the fork that answers its own failure mode'', and whether that coincidence is right is the question — not whether the other branch exists.

!!! ①′ ⚠ And the KEL board is PER-VESSEL, so identity cannot federate

`personaKelBoardDocUrl(nexusPubkey)` mints the ''identifier → current-op-key'' map, and every vessel derives it from ''its own'' key — the node's KEL ring holder and the browser leaf's materialize both pass `vesselIdentity.verifyingKey`. It is a sibling of the crossroads bulletin and the WHO face, and `federation-gate` federates it deliberately, so these boards are ''meant'' to be shared.

⚠ ''The Binding Gate walks a LOCAL replica and HALTS fail-closed on an absent chain.'' So a member on the wrong board does not misread a fellow member — ''it cannot verify them at all''. The grain error compounds from "reads the wrong bulletin" to "refuses the people it is federated with", and it lands squarely on the recovery question: ''a rotation propagates by reaching the board its readers walk''.

!!! ② The counterweight is ALREADY CLOSED at the human scale

The sweep's warning against Fork B — a rogue sub-quorum emitting a rotation nobody intended, //"closable only by making the rotation body canonically derived"// — is closed here already. `personaRotationSigningBytes(head, freshOpKeyDid)` ''derives'' the signed body from the head and the fresh key; `mintPersonaRotation` recomputes identical bytes to verify. ''Guardians sign a derived body, never a proposed one.''

!!! ③ ⚠ AND THE THIRD AXIS IS OPEN — our identifiers are NOT delegated

```
personaPrefixOf(inceptionOpKeyDid, recoverySetHash)
  → `persona-${sha256(domain, op, recoverySetHash)}`
```

''Self-certifying, with no delegator — and that stands RULED rather than open.'' `identity-classes#the-two-forks` (`:520-529`) priced delegation and REJECTED it for the persona plane: a delegated inception carries the delegator's AID inside the prefix permanently with no severance mechanic, buying recoverability with //a landlord in the identifier//, and the ROLLING COMMITMENT answers the graft without one.

''The residual that ruling names stands BUILT.'' What delegation alone would have bought — superseding a compromise OF the current rolling set between grafts — narrows in that same paragraph to the contest window's job (Fork C: a provisional rotation the true holder out-competes causally), and that machinery runs in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-kel.ts`. The event carries `provisional` (//a recovery rotation NOT yet hardened — kapae-reversible authority only//) and `vetoOfCid`; `personaVetoSigningBytes` + `vetoCore` derive the veto's bytes from the contested and standing events so a veto never floats onto another contest; `mintVeto` takes ONE signature from the STANDING op-key, the holder's own hand being the whole authority the contest exists to protect; `foldPersonaContests` folds veto-beats-provisional into one linear lineage, and `verifyPersonaKelFull` REFUSES a chain whose veto does not verify. Witnessed at `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/provisional-veto.test.ts`. Re-derive: `grep -n "provisional\|vetoOfCid\|mintVeto\|foldPersonaContests" packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-kel.ts`.

''So a recovery-authority compromise reads survivable, not terminal'': a provisional rotation minted by a captured recovery set confers kapae-reversible authority only and dies to the standing holder's own veto.

''What stays terminal runs narrower, and lives elsewhere.'' A LIVE capture of the current op-key ''together with'' its next commitment leaves no standing hand to veto with. `api/pono/field-collision#/standing-tensions` already carries that as THE LIVE-EQUIVOCATOR EXPOSURE, and it falls to recovery-quorum rather than to delegation. The severity read this section made survives — attached to that case, never to the delegation axis.

Read from `persona-kel.ts` and `recovery-keel-core.ts` at their current heads. ⚠ ''Read, not run'' — no vector walks a rotation against live key material at either scale. `provisional-veto.test.ts` walks the grammar; it does not walk live key material.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/how-far-to-trust-this>>

!! How far to trust this sweep

* ''Four claims were corrected in place mid-sweep'', by the spirit against itself — including a material error in the social-recovery figures and a wrong claim that ICANN never wrote down the unavailable-shareholder case. ''A report that corrects itself twice is worth more than one that never does''; read the corrections as the instrument working.
* ''The search budget was exhausted at the first call and general engines were bot-walled.'' Primary documents and academic PDFs were reached and read in full; ''forum evidence and removed pre-2020 pages were not''. So absence of a finding here reads weaker than presence of one.
* ''Thirteen negative results'' are listed, and two carry weight on their own: ''no wallet vendor has ever published a recovery success rate'', and no public-CA record of an unreachable keyholder blocking a signature.
* ''No fork is recommended, deliberately.'' Where the evidence points in opposite directions — the courier property, the strict-versus-relaxed threshold question — both sides stand.

<<~/ahu>>

